  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nTuesday, a northern-stream longwave trough will become established \nacross the Dakotas, driving stronger mid-level cold air advection \nacross the region. Forecast 850-mb temperature anomalies fall to \naround 4 to 6C below normal. At the surface, a cold front will move \nthrough the area Monday night into Tuesday morning, with gusty \nnortheast winds developing behind the boundary. High temperatures \nTuesday will remain quite cool, generally in the upper 60s to lower \n70s, with lingering cloud cover likely limiting daytime heating. In \nterms of precipitation, the cold front is expected to move south \ninto northern and central Kansas, keeping the strongest forcing \nprimarily south of the forecast area. However, some guidance \nsuggests better forcing may extend farther north into southern \nportions of the area, supporting at least some precipitation \npotential Tuesday morning. The exact northern extent of this \nprecipitation remains uncertain. Lows Tuesday night will fall into \nthe middle to upper 40s north, with middle to upper 50s farther \nsouth.\n\nWednesday, westerly flow aloft will become established across the \nregion as another disturbance approaches within the flow. Meanwhile, \nthe surface front is expected to remain nearly stationary across \nKansas. Temperatures will remain relatively cool, with highs \ngenerally in the middle 70s to lower 80s. Precipitation chances will \nincrease as mid-level warm air advection and frontogenesis \nstrengthen north of the stationary boundary. This should promote \nincreasing isentropic ascent and a more overrunning-type \nprecipitation regime, with showers and thunderstorms developing \nacross portions of the region. The greatest precipitation chances \ncurrently appear across southern and eastern portions of the \nforecast area. The surface-based severe weather threat should remain \nlow with the area north of the frontal boundary. However, elevated \ninstability combined with relatively low freezing levels may support \na few stronger thunderstorms. Lows Wednesday night will range from \nthe upper 40s north to lower 50s elsewhere.\n\nThursday and beyond, the pattern will remain active as multiple weak \ndisturbances move through the established westerly flow aloft. \nTemperatures should remain more fall-like, with highs generally in \nthe 70s through the remainder of the period. A persistent baroclinic \nzone is forecast to remain near northern Kansas and southern \nNebraska. Repeated disturbances interacting with this zone should \nsupport periodic precipitation chances through the end of the long-\nterm period. Considerable uncertainty remains regarding the exact \nplacement of the baroclinic zone and timing of individual \ndisturbances, which will influence where the greatest precipitation \namounts ultimately occur. However, confidence is increasing in at \nleast some wetting rainfall across western and north-central \nNebraska during the period. The greatest potential currently appears \ntoward late week as a stronger system approaches the region. \nOverall, a cooler and wetter pattern is expected through the long-\nterm period.",
